1.bug_scenario:
When a JavaScript file consists solely of a directive (e.g. 'use strict'), followed by a blank line and then a comment, Prettier emits two trailing newlines at the end of the formatted output instead of a single newline. In the example, the input

'use strict';

// comment

is formatted as

"use strict";

// comment



(i.e. two blank lines) rather than ending with only one newline. The extra hardline appears to come from the directive printer always appending a hardline plus the final printer logic adding its own newline.

2.bug_scenario:
When formatting a file that contains only a directive (e.g. 'use strict') followed by a blank line and a comment, Prettier (with the babylon parser) ends the file with two newlines instead of one. In our repro the input is:

'use strict';

// comment

and Prettier emits two trailing hardlines (an extra blank line) after the comment.
